A||N||24||Jonathan||Gun Owners Beware||||4||
Z||000000||Jonathan||09-14-2000||10:06 AM||||Gun owners beware!<P><BR>Back Door Bill (SB-2099) Senate Bill SB-2099 will require us to put on our 2000 1040 federal tax Form all guns that you have or own.  It may require fingerprints and a tax of $50 per gun.  This bill was introduced on Feb.  24.<BR>This bill will become public knowledge 30 days after it is voted into law.<BR>This is an amendment to the Internal Revenue Act of 1986.  This means that the Finance Committee can pass this without the Senate voting on it at all.<BR>The full text of the proposed amendment is on the U.S.  Senate homepage: <A HREF="http://www.senate.gov/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.senate.gov/</A>  You can find the<BR>bill by doing a search by the bill number.  (SB-2099) You know who to call; I<BR>strongly suggest you do.  <BR>||||reg||4
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-14-2000||01:28 PM||||I have gotten a lot of bullcrap "Big Brother's gonna steal your guns" emails lately, so I was skeptical - until I went out to <A HREF="http://www.senate.gov/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.senate.gov/</A>  and punched in "SB2099."<P>Jonathan, I'm sorry I doubted you - the bill appears to do exactly what you claim it will, which means it is in direct violation of the 2nd Amendment.<P>I just hope somebody on Capitol Hill cares about enforcing the Bill of Rights. ||||reg||4
Z||000002||Jonathan||09-14-2000||10:55 PM||||I wrote to both of my States Senators.  May not do any good, but if this bill passes or not, at least I will feel as if I did my part.  Those who are interested should read the Senate page.  The full scope of this bill is not obvious until you read it in its full form.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||09-14-2000||11:05 PM||||OGRE!!!!! What is up with this? How worried should be be right now?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-14-2000||11:53 PM||||If you own hand guns you should probably be quite worried.<P>Personally I don't see how they can enforce it. There are way to many guns of any type to trace as it is. On top of that the NRA and other organizations will have it tied up in federal court the second it passes. Even if this is stopped it's only a matter of time before the seocnd ammendment is gone. <P>Of course this has to be taken in the scope of a multipronged attack on the bill of rights. Have good read of some of the "hate speach" and other B.S. that is supposedly to stop racial or anti gay speach and crime. You also have to look at some of the stuff just passed under the guis of copyright law that screws up the "fair use" rules that have been establitshed for decades at least.<P>Then add to that the giant invasions of privacy that go on every day, many of which people sign up for..... Grocery Store "bonus cards, and movie rentals, where they track every thing you buy rent etc with these cards.<P>Cookies from webs sites or even thru HTML E-mail. Carparts.com loves cookies in E-mail. And don't forget most ad banners, DoubleClick isn't the only one doing that.<P>Don't forget that the feds are hell bent on installing yet more software to monitor internet traffic. <P>Add to that all the cammeras going up. Do you really think the only think they use traffic cameras for is looking for traffic jams. Add to that cameras setup to monitor "high crime" areas with zery good zoom lenses and usually night vision. Cameras to catch people running red lights that can easily be used to monitor every car passing thru an intersection.<P>Lets not forget the feds can hold you in solitary for nearly a year with no trial just because they think you are a spy. Or sneak up on your house and shoot your pets and family because you bought/sold a couple illegal shotguns.<P>The general population is letting the government do what ever it feels like. (Not that it can really be stopped.) The government should do something about this that and the other thing. Never mind that the ONLY reason you get accidental shootings is because the owner is a moron. Blame the gun. Nevermind criminals are already barred from owning guns. Blame the ligitimate owners and treat them like crooks and use the NICS to track them. Meanwhile the crooks with guns get away with it more times than not.<P>The american public is generally stupid. They want what they think is security. They want the government to give it to them. They don't know or care what they give up to get it.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||09-15-2000||10:40 AM||||<BR>Ogre, must agree with what you said.  I am a freedom loving individual, and have great pride in being American. Yet, there is much that has happened in the last eight years that has taken much of our liberty away.<P>People are not allowed to pray, or teach Christ, let alone, you cannot purchase a gun from wally world without a back ground check.  We have to get permits to build a shed, dig a trench, hang a lamp post, or just about any other thing.  And this idea pervades all of our society from the government to businesses.  For just the other day, I purchased a new computer which did not include a cd-rw so I purchased one and installed it.<P>I had problems with the OS not the cd-rw and the guy behind the counter says:<BR>"Are you a qualified technition or did you have it installed by a qualified technition?" I said, "No." And he informs me "That voids your warranty."  And I proceded to inform him that I have worked on computers for years and everything is plug-n-play anymore when it comes to pc's, and that just because I am not "certified" does not mean that I can't do something.<P>This type of specialized mentality drives me absolutely crazy.  I have the ability to read, to understand, and with that comes the ability to apply what I know, don't tell me I can't do it.  If the founders of the U.S., or the settlers, or those who made automobiles, or those who went to the moon, or those who developed computers had that type of thought the U.S. would not exist and much of the rest of the world would not benefit from our trail blazing.<P>I am not bragging that the U.S. is the best, it clearly has had and still has its problems, but when compared to the rest of the world's governments it's difficult to find a country that let's the poor become rich and the rich lose their wealth, and the other non-material ways in which the U.S. prospers.<P>Many of the current philosophy's being taught in the U.S. are not traditional work your butt off and prosper ideas, they are give me your wealth because I'm a this or that (plug in your favorite catch phrase).  I'm special, I'm handicapped, I burned my self with your products, so society owes me.<P>I and society owe these people nothing.  If you work and work hard and you cannot prosper then you deserve someone's help to help you prosper.  Like the proverbial phrase "Give a man a fish and he eats, teach him how to fish and you feed him for a lifetime."<P>Security and safety are mythical and really do not exist, and never will utopia exist because people are humans and with humanity comes trash.  And the good, honest, hard working, law abiding citizens should be able to defend themselves against the scum of society.<P>Circa 1930-1939 Nazi Germany, sold their citizens a bill of goods about safety. Register your guns, turn in your guns, where are your papers for traveling, ectera, ectera.  All of this was done for the sake of "safety and prosperity" in 1930 Germany and what happened to the people? They were squashed by a tyrant.<P>There are a few who learn from the past, but the vast majority of humanity waits for something to happen to them before they will do anything. It makes me wonder if people ever study and learn from history.<P>Thanks for letting me vent, I'll get off my soap box now.<P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 09-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DJRice||09-15-2000||02:37 PM||||If you are nieve enough to actually fill out that form, maybe you deserve to have your guns taken away.<P>&lt;sarcasm&gt;<BR>Geez, think of the amount of jobs that would be created if the government had to staff a "Men In Black" Division to take all the evil firearms away.  That would be good for the economy....<BR>&lt;/sarcasm&gt;<BR><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||09-15-2000||09:06 PM||||This gun issue really ticks me off.  The liberals have to be the stupidest people in the entire world.  Don't they understand how dangerous a "gun registry" could be? <P>Obviously not, I see all of those movie stars preaching about it a lot, must be nice when you have a small army of body guards (with guns) protecting you and your family.  I was also shocked to learn that in my VERY liberal, one party state of Maryland, I can't even own a gun until I am 21.  So basically they are saying they are willing to send me off in the middle of some foreign countries civil war at 18 yrs. old, but they won't let me exercise my own Constitutional right to own a gun.  If the founding fathers could see us now....<P>BTW the 2nd amendment cannot be repealed since it is in the Bill of Rights, however as we see they can infringe on it as much as they please.  Luckily we have an org. like the NRA defending us.<P>One day hopefully they will realize that there are much much more dangerous problems in this country, and they will stop bothering legal gun owners.<P>But when you have Rosie O'Donnel and other anti-gun losers plastered all over &lt;liberal&gt; TV, public opinion will be swayed in their favor.  Thats the real problem, the media...<P><BR>thats my 2 cents...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||8
Z||000008||Monkeyman||09-15-2000||09:22 PM||||Gold86SE--Not that it matters but you don't need a background check to purchase anything at Wally World (assuming you are referring to WalFart...ummm, I mean WalMart).  They don't sell hand guns, only rifles and shotguns (and BB/pellet guns which don't really count).  You only get a background check for hand guns.  Wally World just requires a picture ID to prove that you're over 18.  <P>For what it's worth, I agree with most of what is being said.  I don't currently own any handguns (just sold the last one) but I plan on purchasing a couple in the near future.  Indiana is one of the easiest state to get a PPP (Personal Protection Permit or Personal Pan Pizza).  $37 and a quick trip to the local cop shop on a Sunday morning pretty much does it.  It doesn't even matter if you have a felony record.  Personally, I think that's stupid.  If you have any violent crimes on your record, you shouldn't be allowed to own any gun let alone an easily concealable one.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 09-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||LarryB||09-15-2000||11:01 PM||||Several states allow convicted felons to buy firearms, usually after some period (like 5 years) after being released from jail, but federal law prohibits it for life unless the felon is specifically exempted and given a waiver by the Attorney General. Federal law supercedes any state law.  <P>Shifting a bit, I remember about 10 years ago or so when Ted Kennedy's private bodyguard was arrested in DC and charged with illegal posession of a handgun. Uncle Teddy pulled some strings and got him off.   ||||reg||1
