Z||000016||Monkeyman||09-16-2000||07:41 PM||||I was pretty sure that the Brady Bill didn't apply to rifles (maybe high powered ones) or shotguns.  Only to handguns, but I won't argue about it.  I'm not an expert and it doesn't make enough difference to me to search for the correct answer.  When the time comes for me to buy another handgun, I'll do whatever the law requires.  I'm well over 21 and I've never been in trouble with the law (other than a few speeding tickets) so I won't have any problems anyway.  Maybe I should buy one soon before this new law goes into effect.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Gold-86SE||09-16-2000||11:23 PM||||<BR>Just received the following email, you may find it interesting in suppporting Jonathan's original post.<P>Before you read this, beware that this proposed legislation is just like every other thing the clinton administration has done, if the legislative body will not do what I want I will find a way around it, through Executive Order, or by any other means.<P>This bill clearly violates the second amendment.<P><BR>September 16, 2000<P><BR>Dear friend of liberty,<P>Senator Jack Reed (RI) introduced S. 2099 in the U.S. Senate on February 24, 2000.  Senators Charles Schumer (NY) and Frank Lautenberg (NJ) are cosponsors of the bill.<P>S. 2099 would amend the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 to require the registration of all handguns, both newly-purchased and currently-owned handguns, with the federal government.  Current owners would be required to register their handguns by submitting an application with a photograph and fingerprints and pay a tax within one year after the enactment of S. 2099.  The information submitted by owners of handguns would be kept in a central-government database known as the National Firearms Registration and Transfer Record maintained by the Secretary of the Treasury.<P>Any law-abiding citizen who owns a handgun and does not want to be tracked by this government database had better carefully read the "Transfer Rule" section of this bill, which states he or she "shall register such handgun"..."upon the transfer of such handgun before such 1 year anniversary date."  In other words, you will be required to register handguns you no longer own.<P>Gotcha!<P>Gun makers would be assessed a $50.00 tax per gun manufactured. This on the manufacturer, as do all such taxes on the production of goods, will be passed on to the consumer as an increase in the purchase price.<P>S. 2099 is currently in the U.S. Senate Committee on Finance.  We need to make sure that S. 2099 doesn't make it out of committee.<P>Even though S. 2099 has only two cosponsors, it is, nonetheless, a pending bill.  As I wrote in my last update, anything is possible during the last weeks of a congressional session.  Therefore, we need to tell our U.S. senators we are aware of S. 2099 and that we strongly oppose it.<P>Go to <A HREF="http://www.thelibertycommittee.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thelibertycommittee.org</A>   and click on S. 2099 in the Self Defense section, then click on Take Action Here! to express your opinion about S. 2099 to your two U.S. senators.<P>Kent Snyder<BR>The Liberty Committee <A HREF="http://www.thelibertycommittee.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thelibertycommittee.org</A>  <P>SUBSCRIBE and UNSUBSCRIBE directions:<P>To SUBSCRIBE, please go to <A HREF="http://www.thelibertycommittee.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thelibertycommittee.org</A>   and click Join.<P>To UNSUBSCRIBE, please<BR>Mailto:majordomo-libertystudycommittee@mjx.libertystudy.org<BR>with the words<BR>unsubscribe LibertyStudyCommittee<P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)<P>Note:<BR>Edited for improper placed returns, the copy/paste function did not perform as well as expected.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 09-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||olympic||09-16-2000||11:51 PM||||  Yeah, here in Canada were going through gun registration already.  But it's ALL guns they want registered now.  Automatic and assault rifles have always been illegal and hand guns might as well be illegal because of how regulated they are.  Now they are going after rifles and shotguns spending millions of dollars to lay a piece of paper beside every rifle which wont stop one single crime.  In fact it will turn law abiding citizens into criminals for not registering their guns!!<P>  Until now it was a pretty fair system.  You had to take a gun safety course before you could apply for a Firearms Aquasition Certificate(FAC).   Once you passed the course you  could to apply for the FAC. The police did a criminal records check and you even needed to supply references.  But once you had the FAC you could buy as many rifles/shotguns as you wanted.<P>Now, with the new registration law you need a license(requires a yearly fee) to even own a gun or purchase ammunition.  And you have to register all guns and pay a fee(of course)  And I'm betting there will be a yearly fee involved too.  They'll simply tax guns out of existance!!<P>   There's one part of this new law that should scare the crap out of all Canadians.  This law gives the police the right to search your home at any time on the basis that they "suspect"  you have unregistered guns inside.  Unbelievable!!!  Our only hope is the RCMP which has been hinting that they might withdraw their support for this law because it is not effective and will waste valuable police manpower in doing endless paperwork.<P>  I remember my highschool history teacher asking us "Why do you think Hitler never invaded Switzerland ?"  Because it is the law in Switzerland that every household have an automatic weapon and all male adults are trained to use them.  Makes sense doesn't it. ||||reg||4
Z||000019||KitKar||09-17-2000||09:26 PM||||According to whatever law was passed a year or 2 ago, a background check must be completed for all firearms purchases.<BR>Also, beware of the "since this law has been in effect, we have kept a bazillion guns out of criminals hands" claims. I sell knives and watches at local gun shows and have talked to many gun dealers. If you have an unpaid traffic ticket, you will fail the check. Each time that happens another number is added to the "Criminals Hands" statistics.<BR>Here in AZ the gun laws are not too bad. Anybody can carry one as long as it is not concealed. It is easy to get a concealed permit too. I carry a concealed handgun everyday.<P>------------------<BR>Rick Lord<BR>Webmaster<BR>http://www.KitKar.com||||reg||1
Z||000020||LarryB||09-17-2000||09:35 PM||||Ugh.  Anything with Chuck Schumer's fingerprints on it just cries out BAD BILL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>This is another good reason why any gun owner who isn't yet a member of the NRA <A HREF="http://www.nra.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.nra.org</A>  should consider joining now, especially if you belong to an NRA-affliated gun club.  You (like me) may not always agree with what they say or do, but they're one of the effective voices out there that work in our behalf. <P>As for Monkeyman's comments re: Brady, it does indeed include the purchase of rifles and shotguns as well as handguns.  It also only covers those (31?) states that don't have their own approved pre-certification program that includes background checks.  For example, Brady applies in Maine, it doesn't in Massachusetts. <P><BR>||||reg||1
